   "final_text": "Hana Nguyen moved from contractor to employee, and her existing account now belongs to her new employee record. The feed had three events and all are handled. It has been quiet for about 50 minutes and nothing I started is still pending.\n\n**What came in:**\n- **Seq 1, case-d9bc1d97:** Termination of contractor record C5001 (Hana Nguyen), effective 2026-11-02. Before taking anything away, I checked for a new record. Employee E1023 started the same day, is marked as converted from C5001 and has the same government ID token. So this was a conversion, not someone leaving. Our policy for conversions is to carry the existing identity over.\n- **Seq 2:** The email-change confirmation my own write-back produced. Nothing to do.\n- **Seq 3, case-f9495568:** The hire event for E1023, which arrived after I'd already done the conversion work. Nothing more was needed.\n\n**What I did:**\n- **Identity:** Moved account acct-4f73fd94 (hana.nguyen@example.com) to the E1023 record and changed its type to Employee. She keeps the same sign-in, email address, mailbox, Office-E3 license and history. Her groups (All-Staff, Eng-Git, VPN-Users) already match the standard set for a Software Engineer. I didn't disable the account, end her sessions or touch her mailbox, so her access never dropped.\n- **Finance:** Created a finance profile for E1023 with cost center CC-1000 and Avery Carter as expense approver. Her old contractor profile has no active card and no open expense reports, so there was nothing to close.\n- **Workday:** Wrote hana.nguyen@example.com back to E1023 and confirmed it saved. I marked both onboarding tasks Complete.\n- **Equipment and legal:** She keeps her engineering laptop, so I didn't open a recovery ticket or order another one. There is no legal hold on her.\n\n**Decisions you may want to check:**\n- **Equipment task:** That task normally counts as done once a new order is placed. I marked it Complete with no new order, because her laptop from her contractor days carries over (ticket tkt-eq-C5001, already delivered).\n- **Final pay:** I didn't request final pay for the contractor record. Final pay can't be undone, and it isn't clearly required for a contractor who is staying on as an employee.\n\nNo escalations or fix requests were needed."
